# SYNOPSIS

```swift
import Themes

struct ThemeStack {
  init(theme: String, variant: String? = nil,
       layers: [ThemeLayer] = [], highContrast: Bool = false,
       knobs: [(name: String, value: String)] = [])
}
struct ThemeLayer { init(label: String, text: [UInt8]) }

struct Theme {
  static func resolve(_ stack: ThemeStack,
                      installed: (String) -> [UInt8]?)
    throws(ThemeError) -> Theme
  static let base: Theme

  let name: String
  let title: String
  let textScale: Float
  let knobs: [Knob]
  func style(_ name: String) -> Style
  func color(_ role: String) -> UInt32
  func typeface(_ role: FontRole) -> Typeface
  func knob(_ name: String) -> String?
}

struct ThemeError: Error { var file: String; var line: Int
                           var message: String; var text: String }

enum ThemeCheck {
  static func check(_ theme: String, layers: [ThemeLayer] = [],
                    installed: (String) -> [UInt8]?) -> [String]
}

enum Contrast {
  static func ratio(_ a: UInt32, _ b: UInt32) -> Double
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

Themes reads theme files (theme(6)) and resolves a stack of them to one `Theme`, what UICore, the decorations and the apps draw in.

`resolve` takes the theme by name, a variant, the layers over it from the user's up, whether high contrast is on, and knob settings over everything; `installed` gives a theme's file by its name, and the base needs none.
It merges the stack by name, takes borrowed parts from their lenders' chains, then evaluates each part against the theme's roles.
An error is a `ThemeError` naming the file (a theme's name or a layer's label) and line; `text` is `file:line: message`.

A `Theme` is the result: a `Style` per class, with dot fallback (`button.primary`, else `button`, else `default`) and a pressed `.held` derived where the theme has none; a colour per role, 0xAARRGGBB, or magenta for a role it lacks; a `Typeface` per font role; and its knobs with their values.
A `Style` is a fill, a stroke and an ink, a border, a radius, corner cuts, a glow and its colour, a font role, weight, size, line height, tracking, case, padding, and materials.

`ThemeCheck.check` is theme check: each variant, and each knob at its extremes, against the floor of theme(6); its findings are lines of text, and none means the theme passes.
`Contrast.ratio` is WCAG 2.2's contrast of two colours.

